sec.p:
Given above information, the set of possible values of the number of goals for a single team is defined as follows:G={0,1,2,…,Gmax}
        and the sample space of all possible final scores of the match is a Cartesian product:Ω=G×G={(x,y)∣x∈G,y∈G}={(x,y)∣0≤x,y≤Gmax},
		where x and y denote the scored goals for the home and away teams, respectively.

To help with intuition, here are some examples of groups with two mutually exclusive outcomes that are common in football betting:
“Home team win” and “Draw or Away team win” (double chance);
“Total goals under 2.5” and “Total goals over 2.5”;
“Both teams to score—Yes” and “Both teams to score—No”;
“Home team to win with handicap +1.5” and “Away team to win with handicap −1.5”;
etc.

Let:
B denote the set of all observed bets with known decimal odds corresponding to a single match;
Ibet(x,y) be an indicator function of bet bet∈B, equal to 1 if outcome (x,y)∈Ω results in a win for a given bet, and 0 otherwise;
Abet={(x,y)∈Ω∣Ibet(x,y)=1}, i.e., the set of outcomes for which the bet wins;
P(x,y) be the unknown estimate of true probability for final score (x,y) corresponding to a single match;
p^bet be an estimate of the win probability for bet bet, obtained from the market odds of a mutually exclusive group of outcomes corresponding to a single match, using one of the methods described in the previous subsection.

As shown in Figure 5, the proposed method is the best in terms of the smallest bias and MSE, indicating that its expected profit is the closest to the actual observed cumulative profit on a dataset of over three million bets. This suggests that our probability estimates closely approximate the internal probabilities used by the bookmaker when creating odds, effectively removing the margin to recover the implied true probabilities.
Algorithm 1 Monte Carlo estimation of profits distribution
Require: List of odds and method’s probability estimates (oi,pi) for all bets
Ensure: Sample of Nsim simulated profits based on estimated probabilities1:
Nsim←1000 ▷ Resulting sample size2:
simulated_expected_profits ← []3:
for trial_num = 1 to Nsim do4:
curr_profit ← 0 ▷ Start new simulation from 0 profit5:
for each (Oi,p^i) in all bets do6:
Draw u∼U(0,1)7:
if u≤p^i then8:
Δprofit←1−1/Oi ▷ Bet is considered as a winning bet9:
else10:
Δprofit←−1/Oi ▷ Bet is considered as a losing bet11:
end if12:
curr_profit ← curr_profit + Δprofit13:
end for14:
Append curr_profit to simulated_profits ▷ Save one simulation result15:
end for16:
return simulated_profits ▷ Return distribution of N simulated profits
